The '''2009 UEFA Cup Final''' was the final match of the [[2008–09 UEFA Cup]], the 38th season of the [[UEFA Cup]], [[UEFA]]'s second-tier club [[association football|football]] tournament. It was also the last final to be held under the UEFA Cup name, as the competition was rebranded as the UEFA Europa League from the [[2009–10 UEFA Europa League|2009–10 season]]. The final was contested by [[FC Shakhtar Donetsk|Shakhtar Donetsk]] and [[SV Werder Bremen|Werder Bremen]], with Shakhtar winning the match 2–1 after [[Overtime (sports)#Association football|extra time]]. All three goalscorers in the game were Brazilians; lone striker [[Luiz Adriano]] opened the scoring for Shakhtar midway through the first half, before [[Naldo (footballer, born 1982)|Naldo]] equalised from a free kick ten minutes later. The second half was goalless and the match went to extra time; after only seven minutes, [[Jádson]] scored for Shakhtar to secure the club's first major European trophy.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2009/may/20/shakhtar-donetsk-uefa-cup-werder|title=Shakhtar Donetsk claim Uefa Cup final glory over Werder Bremen|date=21 May 2009|work=The Guardian|access-date=11 August 2009|archive-date=30 July 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170730065441/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2009/may/20/shakhtar-donetsk-uefa-cup-werder|url-status=live}}</ref>
[[File:Кубок ЮЕФА В музеи Домбасс Арены - panoramio.jpg|alt=|thumb|276x276px|UEFA Cup trophy on display at the [[Donbas Arena]] museum.]]
The match was played at the [[Şükrü Saracoğlu Stadium]] – home ground of [[Fenerbahçe S.K. (football)|Fenerbahçe]] – in [[Istanbul]], Turkey, on 20 May 2009.<ref>{{Cite web |first=Mark |last=Chaplin |title=Moscow chosen for 2008 final |url=http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/news/kind=1/newsid=464106.html |access-date=28 August 2007 |date=4 October 2006 |publisher=UEFA |archive-date=12 March 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120312201736/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/index.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> It was the second European football final to be held in Turkey, after the [[2005 UEFA Champions League Final]], which was held in another Istanbul venue, the [[Atatürk Olympic Stadium]]. It was also the first European football final to be held outside the geographical Europe, as Şükrü Saracoğlu Stadium lies across the [[Bosphorus]] from the city centre, and hence, in geographical Asia.

Former Fenerbahçe player [[Can Bartu]] was appointed as ambassador of the final.

==Background==
The 2009 UEFA Cup final marked the first meeting between [[FC Shakhtar Donetsk|Shakhtar Donetsk]] and [[SV Werder Bremen|Werder Bremen]], although Shakhtar had previously played nine matches against German sides (won 4, lost 3), while Werder had played just four matches against Ukrainian teams (won 2, lost 1). Both teams were playing in their first UEFA Cup final, and Shakhtar were playing in their first final in any of the three major UEFA competitions; Werder Bremen beat [[AS Monaco FC|Monaco]] 2–0 in the [[1992 European Cup Winners' Cup Final]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Shakhtar and Bremen in Istanbul showdown |url=http://en.archive.uefa.com/competitions/uefacup/fixturesresults/round=15290/match=302827/report=mb.html |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120708004936/http://en.archive.uefa.com/competitions/uefacup/fixturesresults/round=15290/match=302827/report=mb.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=8 July 2012 |publisher=UEFA |date=20 May 2009 |access-date=11 April 2011 }}</ref>

==Venue==
The 2009 UEFA Cup final also marked the first time that the [[Şükrü Saracoğlu Stadium]] had hosted a major European final, as well as being the first time that the UEFA Cup final had been held in Turkey;<ref>{{Cite news|title=Şükrü Saracoğlu Stadium |url=http://www.uefa.com/competitions/uefacup/finals/newsid=739122.html |publisher=UEFA |date=20 March 2009 |access-date=25 May 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090415173000/http://www.uefa.com/competitions/uefacup/finals/newsid%3D739122.html |archive-date=15 April 2009 |url-status=dead }}</ref> the [[2005 UEFA Champions League Final]] was held at the [[Atatürk Olympic Stadium]] across the [[Bosporus]] from the Şükrü Saracoğlu.<ref>{{cite news|first=Adam |last=Szreter |title=Liverpool triumph in Turkey |url=http://www.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/history/season=2004/round=1972/match=1086988/index.html |publisher=UEFA |date=25 May 2005 |access-date=25 May 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090525103914/http://www.uefa.com/Competitions/UCL/history/Season%3D2004/Round%3D1972/match%3D1086988/index.html |archive-date=25 May 2009 |url-status=dead }}</ref> It is the only European final to not be played in Europe, as the Şükrü Saracoğlu Stadium is technically in Asia.

